# Accessibility

## Semantic HTML

Prefer semantic elements:
- header
- nav
- main
- section
- article
- footer
- button
- form
- label

Do not use clickable divs when a button or link is correct.

## Keyboard navigation

All interactive elements must be keyboard accessible.

Focus states must remain clearly visible.
Do not remove browser focus outlines without providing a strong equivalent.

## Navigation

The menu must:
- be keyboard accessible
- trap focus appropriately when implemented as a modal/drawer
- close with Escape where appropriate
- expose clear names to assistive technology

## Forms

Every field must have an accessible label.
Errors must be associated with the relevant field.
Do not rely only on color to show errors.

## Images

Use meaningful alt text for informative images.
Use empty alt text for purely decorative images.
Do not stuff keywords into alt text.

## Contrast

Text and controls must maintain readable contrast.
Do not sacrifice readability for brand colors.

## Motion

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
Avoid essential information that exists only inside animation.

## Touch targets

Interactive mobile controls must be comfortably tappable.

## Screen readers

Use visually hidden labels where needed.
Do not add ARIA when semantic HTML already provides the behavior.
Use ARIA to enhance semantics, not to compensate for poor HTML.
